    "result_text": "Done. The change is implemented and the test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test run, 0 failures).\n\n## What I changed\n\n**`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 the membership logic lives here as small, cohesive default methods, so I kept the work local and reused what already existed:\n\n- Added a new small `membershipPoints(Owner)` method implementing the points system: `+2` email, `+1` when `namesakeCount == 0`, `+2` for a household of 3+ (`householdMemberCount >= 3`), `+3` for tenure over 365 days. It reuses the existing `tenureDays` helper rather than duplicating date logic.\n- Rewrote `membershipLevel(Owner)` to simply map points \u2192 level: `1` (0-1), `2` (2-3), `3` (4-5), `4` (6+). It now delegates to `membershipPoints`, so the two fields can't drift.\n- Added one `@Mapping` line so the DTO exposes `membershipPoints` alongside `membershipLevel`.\n\n**`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `membershipPoints` property (drives the generated DTO field) and updated the `membershipLevel` description/`maximum` (now 4) to match the new derivation.\n\n## Structural-impact notes\n\n- Only **2 files** touched (the spec, which must change to add the field, and the mapper).\n- New behaviour went into a **new small method** rather than fattening an existing one; each changed function stays simple and reuses `tenureDays`, so there's no duplication and no dead code.\n- `membershipLevel` was reduced to a flat lookup, keeping its complexity low.\n\nThe acceptance test confirms the worked example: a fresh owner with an email, no namesake, single-member household, zero tenure \u2192 3 points \u2192 level 2.",
